@@ -0,0 +1,31 @@
+---
+id: dl-006-applymergepatch-follows-the-rfc-7386-algorithm-null-removes-a-member-objects-merge-recursively-any-other-patch-value-replaces-the-target
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyMergePatch follows the RFC 7386 algorithm: null removes a member, objects merge recursively, any other patch value replaces the target"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+The team's clients send partial updates in JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386). The standard defines the result
+by one algorithm, MergePatch(Target, Patch). The other projects import this library and rely on its
+behaviour following the published standard (README).
+
+## Decision
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` implements the RFC 7386 algorithm as written:
+
+- If `patch` is not an object (including an array), the result is `patch` itself. The target is ignored.
+- If `patch` is an object and `target` is not an object (or is an array), `target` is replaced by an
+  empty object before the members are applied.
+- For each member of `patch`: a `null` value removes that member from the target if it exists; any other
+  value is merged into the target member of the same name, recursively, using the same algorithm.
+- Arrays are not merged element by element: an array in the patch replaces the target value wholesale.
+- `null` nested inside an array is a value, not a removal.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Partial updates behave as the standard specifies, so clients and the other projects agree on results.
+- A patch cannot set a member to `null`; that is inherent to the format and must be documented for callers.
+- Callers wanting element-wise array edits must use `applyPatch` (RFC 6902) instead.
