@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+---
+id: dl-007-applymergepatch-never-mutates-its-inputs-and-writes-only-own-members-so-a-key-such-as-proto-is-ordinary-data
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyMergePatch never mutates its inputs and writes only own members, so a key such as __proto__ is ordinary data"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+dl-002 and dl-004 fix the rules for `applyPatch`: the input is never mutated, and the result shares no
+structure with the patch or the input. The same must hold for `applyMergePatch`, whose recursive merge
+could otherwise write into the input's objects. dl-004 and the `applyPatch` code already write members
+with `Object.defineProperty` and read them with `Object.hasOwn`, so that a key such as `__proto__` is data
+and not the prototype.
+
+## Decision
+
+`applyMergePatch` works on a deep copy of the target and clones every value taken from the patch, so the
+result shares no arrays or objects with either input. Members are read as own members only, and written
+with `defineProperty`, so a `__proto__` key in a patch or target is an ordinary member.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The caller's target and patch are never changed, whatever the result.
+- Copying costs a walk over the target and the patch; this is the same cost as `applyPatch` (dl-002).
+- A `__proto__` member in the patch cannot change the prototype of any object.
diff --git a/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-008-the-patch-given-to-applymergepatch-must-be-a-json-value-checked-deeply-before-any-merge-runs.md b/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-008-the-patch-given-to-applymergepatch-must-be-a-json-value-checked-deeply-before-any-merge-runs.md
